Title: What does O(log n) mean exactly?.
Body: <p>I am learning about Big O Notation running times and amortized times.  I understand the notion of <em>O(n)</em> linear time, meaning that the size of the input affects the growth of the algorithm proportionally...and the same goes for, for example, quadratic time <em>O(n<sup>2</sup>)</em> etc..even algorithms, such as permutation generators, with <em>O(n!)</em> times, that grow by factorials.</p>

<p>For example, the following function is <em>O(n)</em> because the algorithm grows in proportion to its input <em>n</em>:</p>

<pre><code>f(int n) {
  int i;
  for (i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i)
    printf("%d", i);
}
</code></pre>

<p>Similarly, if there was a nested loop, the time would be O(n<sup>2</sup>).</p>

<p>But what exactly is <em>O(log n)</em>?  For example, what does it mean to say that the height of a complete binary tree is <em>O(log n)</em>?</p>

<p>I do know (maybe not in great detail) what Logarithm is, in the sense that:  log<sub>10</sub> 100 = 2, but I cannot understand how to identify a function with a logarithmic time.</p>

answer:
```python
class MyClass:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value

    def __hash__(self):
        # A simple hash function that combines the hash values of all attributes
        return hash((id(self), self.value))
```

In this solution, we define a class `MyClass` with an attribute `value`. We override the `__hash__` method to provide a custom implementation. The hash function uses the identity (`id`) of the object and the value itself to produce a hash code. This approach ensures that objects with the same `value` but different identities will have the same hash code, which is important for collections like dictionaries where uniqueness of keys is crucial.

This method is efficient and should work well for most use cases, but it's worth noting that while this approach provides good performance, it may not be suitable for highly sensitive data or complex objects where collision rates could become a problem. In such cases, more sophisticated hash functions might be necessary.

def factorial(n):
    # Base case: if n is 0 or 1, return 1
    if n == 0 or n == 1:
        return 1
    # Recursive case: n * factorial of (n-1)
    else:
        return n * factorial(n-1)

# Example usage
print(factorial(5))  # Output: 120

