Yes, it is possible to change the color of a PNG image using CSS. You can achieve this by creating an overlay element with the same dimensions as your PNG image and setting its background color to the desired color. Then, you can place the original PNG image inside this overlay element.

Here's an example of how you could do this:

```python
# Import necessary libraries
from flask import Flask, render_template

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def index():
    return render_template('index.html')

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)
```

In your HTML template (`templates/index.html`), you would have something like this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Change Color of PNG</title>
    <style>
        .overlay {
            position: absolute;
            top: 0;
            left: 0;
            width: 100%;
            height: 100%;
            background-color: #f0f0f0; /* Choose your desired color here */
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
        }
        .png-image {
            max-width: 300px; /* Adjust as needed */
            margin-top: 50px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="overlay">
        <img src="{{ url_for('static', filename='example.png') }}" alt="Example Image" class="png-image">
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, we have a `.overlay` class that covers the entire page with a semi-transparent background color. Inside the `.overlay`, we have an `<img>` tag that displays our PNG image. The `class="png-image"` attribute ensures that the image scales down if it's larger than the container. This way, you can easily change the color of the PNG image by modifying the background color in the CSS.

To restrict the `<input type="file">` element to accept only image files (like `.jpg`, `.jpeg`, `.png`, `.gif`), you can use JavaScript along with the `accept` attribute of the input element. Here's how you can do it:

```python
# HTML part for the file input
html_code = """
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>File Input Restriction</title>
</head>
<body>
    <form action="/upload" method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data">
        <label for="image-upload">Select an image:</label><br>
        <input type="file" id="image-upload" name="image" accept=".jpg,.jpeg,.png,.gif">
        <button type="submit">Upload</button>
    </form>
</body>
</html>
"""

# JavaScript part to enforce file extension restriction
js_code = """
<script>
    // Function to check if the selected file is an image
    function isImage(file) {
        const imageExtensions = ['.jpg', '.jpeg', '.png', '.gif'];
        return imageExtensions.includes(file.name.split('.').pop());
    }

    // Event listener for the file input change event
    document.getElementById('image-upload').addEventListener('change', function(event) {
        const file = event.target.files[0];
        if (!isImage(file)) {
            alert('Please select an image file (.jpg, .jpeg, .png, .gif)');
            this.value = ''; // Clear the file input
        }
    });
</script>
"""
```

This solution involves creating an HTML form with a file input field that has the `accept` attribute set to only allow image file types. The JavaScript part adds an event listener to the file input field, checking each selected file against the allowed image extensions. If a file that isn't an image is chosen, an alert is displayed, and the file input is cleared. This ensures that only images are accepted for upload.

